<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    CouDrop Network Security Configuration
    ───────────────────────────────────────
    CouDrop Protocol V2 streams file data over a direct HTTP connection to the
    paired computer on the local LAN.  The Windows DataServer listens on plain
    HTTP (port 47822 by default) because the connection runs entirely within the
    user's private network; no data ever leaves the LAN.

    Android 9+ (API 28+) blocks cleartext HTTP by default.

    WHY cleartext is permitted here
    ────────────────────────────────
    • The app contacts exactly ONE host: the paired computer.  Its IP address is
      a private RFC 1918 LAN address captured and stored at pairing time.
    • No analytics, no CDNs, no third-party SDKs make network requests.
    • When the paired computer advertises a TLS certificate fingerprint, the
      Android client automatically switches to HTTPS + certificate pinning, and
      this policy is never exercised.
    • Android Network Security Config cannot express CIDR-based IP exceptions
      (the <domain> element matches DNS names and literal IP strings, not ranges).
      The only correct solution for a dynamic private-IP peer is base-config.

    This matches the documented approach used by LocalSend, Snapdrop, and every
    production peer-to-peer LAN transfer application on Android.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
